@*
    Opening screen — the Frosted Keynote first viewport.

    The hero is one solid sheet floating on the aurora field, and it carries the
    before/after architecture itself, so an architect reader sees the kind of problem
    being solved before reading a word of prose. The verified metrics live in a single
    mono facts line rather than a stat-card band: they skim in one fixation and no card
    grid is spent on them. The award figure keeps the page's only gold.
*@

<section class="sheet hero" aria-label="Introduction">
    <div class="hero__grid">

        <div class="hero__lede">
            @*
                Each line is its own clipping box so the headline can rise line by
                line rather than fading in as one block. The break points are set
                here rather than left to the wrap, so the emphasis lands on
                "constraint" on its own line at every width.
            *@
            <h1 class="hero__headline">
                <span class="line"><span>I remove the</span></span>
                <span class="line"><span class="hero__accent">constraint</span></span>
                <span class="line"><span>that froze the estate.</span></span>
            </h1>

            <p class="hero__pitch">
                Senior .NET engineer working at architecture level. I take the architectural
                decisions on systems people have stopped touching — then write the template,
                the rules, and the workflow that let the next team repeat it.
            </p>

            @*
                One line, not a band of cards. The figures are the skim, and they carry
                their final values in the markup — if the script never runs the correct
                numbers are already on screen, so the count-up can only ever be an
                enhancement, never the difference between a number and a zero. The
                separators are drawn by CSS on the fact they FOLLOW, so a wrap between
                facts never strands a leading middot on the next line.
            *@
            <p class="hero__facts" aria-label="Track record at a glance">
                <span class="hero__fact">upgrade cycle <strong class="mono" data-count-to="3" data-count-from="7">3</strong> days, from 5–7</span>
                <span class="hero__fact"><strong class="mono" data-count-to="9">9</strong> payment markets</span>
                <span class="hero__fact">@ExperiencePhrase</span>
                <span class="hero__fact hero__fact--gold"><strong class="mono" data-count-to="@AwardCount">@AwardCount</strong> awards, one a company award</span>
            </p>

            <div class="hero__actions">
                <a class="btn btn--primary" href="/cv">Read the CV</a>
                <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="#work">Selected work</a>
                @if (!string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(Profile.GitHub))
                {
                    <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="@Profile.GitHub">GitHub</a>
                }
            </div>
        </div>

        <figure class="diagram">
            <figcaption class="mono diagram__label">The decision, in one diagram</figcaption>

            @*
                The diagram's meaning cannot live in its pixels for everyone: this text
                alternative carries the argument — including the blocker, whose point is
                otherwise made only by colour and a dashed border — to screen readers
                and to any visitor the animation never reaches.
            *@
            <p class="visually-hidden">
                Before: three legacy services — .NET Framework 4.5, .NET Core 2.2, and one
                unversioned service — all depend on a Distributed Transaction Coordinator,
                the component pinning them to runtimes that stopped receiving support.
                After: an explicit orchestrator coordinates the same services, modernized,
                with the coordinator removed entirely. The transformation is repeatable:
                a template, rules, and an AI-guided workflow let other teams run it too.
            </p>

            <div class="diagram__body">
                <div class="panel panel--before">
                    <p class="mono panel__label">Before</p>
                    <div class="panel__stack">
                        <span class="node">Service · .NET FW 4.5</span>
                        <span class="node">Service · Core 2.2</span>
                        <span class="node">Service</span>
                        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
                        <span class="node node--blocker">Distributed Transaction Coordinator</span>
                    </div>
                </div>

                <div class="bridge" aria-hidden="true">
                    <span class="bridge__line"></span>
                    <span class="bridge__arrow">→</span>
                </div>

                <div class="panel panel--after">
                    <p class="mono panel__label">After</p>
                    <div class="panel__stack">
                        <span class="node node--lead">Orchestrator</span>
                        <span class="arrow" aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
                        <span class="node">Service · modernized</span>
                        <span class="node">Service · modernized</span>
                        <span class="node">Service</span>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </figure>
    </div>
</section>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required Profile Profile { get; set; }

    [Parameter, EditorRequired]
    public required string ExperiencePhrase { get; set; }

    [Parameter]
    public int AwardCount { get; set; }
}
